The ZIP reader ends a Zstandard entry where its data ends, even if that fills its buffer.

One of this project's patches to libarchive, which every build (macOS, Linux and
Windows) applies with patch_libarchive (native/build-config.sh), to the libarchive
release pinned there, in the order that LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES there lists them. A build
fails if any hunk does not apply exactly (no fuzz), and scripts/check-native-updates.sh
will not propose a libarchive release that it does not apply to. See "The libarchive
patches" in native/BUILD.md.

The ZIP reader (archive_read_support_format_zip.c) decompresses a Zstandard entry
(method 93) with ZSTD_decompressStream(), into a buffer of ZSTD_DStreamOutSize()
bytes, 128 KiB. It ended an entry whose sizes it knows only when
ZSTD_decompressStream() returned 0, had used all the input it was given, and had not
filled that buffer. An entry whose data ends just as the buffer fills, such as one of
128 KiB or of a multiple of 128 KiB, therefore did not end: the next read found none
of its compressed bytes left and failed with "Truncated zstd file body". The reader
knows the sizes of every entry that it reads from a file or other seekable source,
from the central directory, and of an entry whose local header holds them, as
Python's zipfile writes them. It ends an entry whose sizes follow its data
(ZIP_LENGTH_AT_END), as libarchive's own zip writer writes them, wherever
ZSTD_decompressStream() returns 0, so such an entry failed only when read from a
seekable source.

ZSTD_decompressStream() returns 0 once a frame is decoded and all of its data is
flushed, whether or not that fills the buffer. The reader now ends an entry whose
sizes it knows when that happens and none of the entry's compressed bytes are left.
Any that are left hold another frame, which the next read decodes, so an entry of
several frames still reads in full. Such an entry also no longer ends early where one
of its frames ends at the end of the data read so far, which the old test took for
the end of the entry. An entry whose sizes follow its data still ends with its first
frame, as before.

libarchive 3.8.11 and libarchive's master branch (432ab464, 16 November 2026) have
the old test, which came with the reader's Zstandard support (b0ad184f, first in
libarchive 3.6.0).

The change is marked LIBARCHIVE_NET_ZIP_ZSTD_END_OF_ENTRY, the marker that
LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES gives this patch: patch_libarchive checks that every file the patch
changes has it.

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c
+++ b/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c
@@ -2985,7 +2985,16 @@
 
 	/* Check end of the stream. */
 	if (ret == 0) {
-		if ((in.pos == in.size) && (out.pos < out.size)) {
+		/*
+		 * LIBARCHIVE_NET_ZIP_ZSTD_END_OF_ENTRY: ZSTD_decompressStream()
+		 * returns 0 once a frame is decoded and flushed, even if that
+		 * fills the output buffer.  An entry of unknown size
+		 * (ZIP_LENGTH_AT_END) ends with the frame, as above; one of
+		 * known size ends with the frame that uses its last
+		 * compressed byte, and any bytes left hold another frame.
+		 */
+		if ((zip->entry->zip_flags & ZIP_LENGTH_AT_END)
+		    || (int64_t)in.pos == zip->entry_bytes_remaining) {
 			zip->end_of_entry = 1;
 			ZSTD_freeDStream(zip->zstdstream);
 			zip->zstdstream_valid = 0;
